<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>DemoProducts.Api</RootNamespace>

    <PublishAot>true</PublishAot>
    <!-- Already implied by PublishAot; stated so the consequence is visible to the reader. -->
    <TrimMode>full</TrimMode>
    <EnableRequestDelegateGenerator>true</EnableRequestDelegateGenerator>

    <!-- Names the offending METHOD in the publish log instead of collapsing to one IL2104 per assembly. -->
    <TrimmerSingleWarn>false</TrimmerSingleWarn>

    <!--
      ADR 0001, option A. TreatWarningsAsErrors=true (Directory.Build.props) propagates into ILC, which
      then reports the trim/AOT diagnostics as ERRORS and stops the publish.

      There were 139 of them. A package pin took it to 43, and moving the producing side off Confluent's
      Avro serde - and therefore off Newtonsoft.Json - took it to 4. All 4 are Confluent.Kafka:
      Librdkafka.SetDelegates (rooted from AddKafkaProducer), Marshal.PtrToStructure<T>, and two on the
      AWS OAuth wiring this application never configures. NONE come from DemoProducts.*, and none clear
      on their own: Confluent.Kafka 2.15.0's net10.0 asset carries no trim annotations at all.

      The demotion is scoped as narrowly as the tooling allows: this property, this project. It does NOT
      touch the C# compiler warnings, which stay errors, and it adds no <NoWarn> and no
      [UnconditionalSuppressMessage] - the diagnostics are still printed, counted and reviewable.

      It lives here rather than on the publish command line so that the Dockerfile, the README command
      and CI all get the same result from one source of truth. ADR 0001 carries the per-family argument,
      the two trimming defects the produce smoke found, and what replaced the Newtonsoft edges.
    -->
    <IlcTreatWarningsAsErrors>false</IlcTreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!-- A type missing from the JSON context throws instead of quietly reflecting - on the CLR too. -->
    <JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>false</JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Makes `dotnet publish` clean. ILC forwards $(NoWarn) into its own nowarn argument, so setting it
    here silences the four diagnostics ADR 0001 records as terminal. All four are inside
    Confluent.Kafka, none is fixable from this repository, and none comes from DemoProducts.*:

        IL2067  Librdkafka.SetDelegates                     rooted from AddKafkaProducer, proven by
                                                            ablation, so the behaviour is correct
        IL2091  Marshal.PtrToStructure of T                 P/Invoke struct marshalling
        IL2026  AwsAutoWireDispatcher.ResolveCreateHandler  AWS OAuth wiring; this service sets no
        IL2075  AwsAutoWireDispatcher.ResolveCreateHandler  SaslMechanism, so it cannot execute

    Scoped to a TARGET that runs before ILC rather than to a PropertyGroup, on purpose: a project-level
    NoWarn element would also reach the C# compiler and blind this project's own source to IL2026 and
    IL2075. Set inside the ILC step, the source-level analysers keep reporting those as errors, which is
    where OUR code is meant to be caught.

    One flag brings all four back, which is what keeps this honest rather than hidden:

        dotnet publish ... -p:ShowThirdPartyTrimWarnings=true

    The cost is real: a NEW diagnostic under one of these four codes is now silent at publish. The build
    gate is what pays for it. IsAotCompatible plus TreatWarningsAsErrors run the same analysers over
    every src/** project, where a finding is an error rather than a line in a log. Read ADR 0001 before
    widening this list.
  -->
  <Target Name="SilenceTerminalThirdPartyIlcWarnings"
          BeforeTargets="WriteIlcRspFileForCompilation"
          Condition="'$(ShowThirdPartyTrimWarnings)' != 'true'">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);IL2026;IL2067;IL2075;IL2091</NoWarn>
    </PropertyGroup>
  </Target>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DemoProducts.Application\DemoProducts.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DemoProducts.Infrastructure\DemoProducts.Infrastructure.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.AspNetCore" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
